pydebuprep (0.15.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The first release of what was meant to be 0.15.0: that tag exists, but a
    test stopped its package from building, so nothing was published.
  * Reports compared with a snapshot label each finding: new since the
    snapshot, worse than at the snapshot, or already there at the snapshot, in
    every format, with a count in the header. Severity and exit codes don't
    change.
  * snapshot now runs every check to record what it finds, so it takes as long
    as a report (debsums can take many minutes) and has the same agreed side
    effects. --no-findings or snapshot_findings: false leaves it out.
  * UPGRADE THE PORTAL FIRST: reports can now carry the new field
    since_snapshot, which portals before 0.11.0 refuse. Reports without a
    snapshot taken by this version don't carry it.
  * New check upgrade-log: what APT, dpkg and Ubuntu's release upgrader logged
    since the snapshot, or in the last 30 days: failed package scripts, errors,
    interrupted runs, removals, and logs that can't say.
  * New check boot-config: an initramfs for each kernel; with a snapshot, the
    kernel command line and Secure Boot.
  * New check package-config: alternatives that point to nothing; with a
    snapshot, APT pinning, foreign architectures, diversions and alternatives.
  * New check storage-health: software RAID, encrypted devices, swap, ZFS and
    Btrfs, read from /proc, /sys and /etc.
  * New check oom-events: processes killed for lack of memory this boot.
  * obsolete-packages sums up version changes since the snapshot, and warns of
    older versions and of packages installed by hand that were removed or are
    now marked automatic. A new option, protected_packages, makes it critical
    if a package you list isn't installed.
  * It's now clear in the documentation, and at a terminal before it runs, that
    debsums can take many minutes. The limitations page says what is tested and
    what a pass proves.

 -- Jostein Elvaker Haande <jehaande@gmail.com>  Sat, 07 Nov 2026 01:01:55 +0100

pydebuprep (0.14.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Security fixes; upgrade if hosts run with configs from a portal or have
    other users. An advisory follows on GitHub.
  * Files other users control are opened one directory at a time, refusing
    symlinks on the way, and a link into /proc, /sys or /dev is refused.
  * Matching a config's log patterns stops after a minute, and only that check
    fails.
  * PYDEBUPREP_ variables, the upload token among them, are no longer passed
    to the commands pydebuprep runs, and a command running as another user
    gets only PATH.
  * Passwords, tokens and keys are redacted from what a report quotes, by the
    shape of what is usually secret. Treat reports as private all the same.
  * Mount points in suggested commands are quoted, and text-direction
    characters are removed from reports.
  * Report, snapshot and saved-config files that are nested too deeply or hold
    numbers too long to read are refused with a clear error.
  * debsums' timeout_seconds may be at most 7200; a config with more is now
    rejected.
  * confluence-legacy's TODO table colours its severity with {color}: the
    status macro's | can end a table cell.
  * The documentation has a check matrix: everything each check looks at, and
    which parts always run and which are opt-in.
  * The report format is unchanged: portal 0.3.0 and newer take these
    reports.

